## 9. S3 Blob Cleanup (Storage-Side)

Harbor's GC has two phases: **DB cleanup** (removes metadata) and **blob deletion from storage** (removes the actual layers from S3). If the GC ran with `--delete-untagged` but the S3 blobs weren't purged, they stay on disk/S3 consuming storage.

### Check S3 bucket size and object count
```bash
# Total size and object count
aws s3 ls s3://<harbor-bucket-name> --recursive --summarize | tail -3

# Size by top-level prefix (docker/registry/v2/blobs vs repositories)
aws s3 ls s3://<harbor-bucket-name>/docker/registry/v2/blobs/ --recursive --summarize | tail -3
aws s3 ls s3://<harbor-bucket-name>/docker/registry/v2/repositories/ --recursive --summarize | tail -3
```

### CloudWatch metrics (if enabled)
```bash
# Bucket-level metrics (requires S3 request metrics or Storage Lens)
aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics \
  --namespace AWS/S3 \
  --metric-name BucketSizeBytes \
  --dimensions Name=BucketName,Value=<harbor-bucket-name> Name=StorageType,Value=StandardStorage \
  --start-time $(date -u -d '7 days ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S) \
  --end-time $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S) \
  --period 86400 \
  --statistics Average

aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics \
  --namespace AWS/S3 \
  --metric-name NumberOfObjects \
  --dimensions Name=BucketName,Value=<harbor-bucket-name> Name=StorageType,Value=AllStorageTypes \
  --start-time $(date -u -d '7 days ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S) \
  --end-time $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S) \
  --period 86400 \
  --statistics Average
```

### Find orphan blobs in S3 (blobs on S3 but not in DB)

After DB GC completes, cross-reference what's in S3 vs what the DB still references:

```bash
# List all blob digests in S3
aws s3 ls s3://<harbor-bucket-name>/docker/registry/v2/blobs/sha256/ --recursive \
  | awk '{print $4}' | grep '/data$' \
  | sed 's|docker/registry/v2/blobs/sha256/||;s|/data$||;s|/||' \
  | sort > /tmp/s3_blobs.txt

# Count
wc -l /tmp/s3_blobs.txt
```

Then from inside the DB pod:
```sql
-- Export DB blob digests
\copy (SELECT digest FROM blob) TO '/tmp/db_blobs.txt'
```

```bash
# Compare — blobs in S3 but NOT in DB are orphans
comm -23 /tmp/s3_blobs.txt <(sort /tmp/db_blobs.txt) > /tmp/orphan_blobs.txt
wc -l /tmp/orphan_blobs.txt
```

### Harbor registry garbage-collect (the storage-side GC)

Harbor's GC via the API/UI handles both DB and storage. But if you need to force the storage-side cleanup, the registry itself has a built-in GC:

```bash
# Exec into the registry pod
kubectl exec -it -n harbor <harbor-registry-pod> -- sh

# Dry run first — shows what would be deleted
registry garbage-collect /etc/registry/config.yml --dry-run

# Actual delete
registry garbage-collect /etc/registry/config.yml --delete-untagged
```

### Add S3 lifecycle rule to auto-expire incomplete uploads

Multipart uploads that fail leave orphan fragments. Add a lifecycle rule:

```bash
aws s3api put-bucket-lifecycle-configuration \
  --bucket <harbor-bucket-name> \
  --lifecycle-configuration '{
    "Rules": [
      {
        "ID": "AbortIncompleteMultipartUploads",
        "Status": "Enabled",
        "Filter": {},
        "AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload": {
          "DaysAfterInitiation": 3
        }
      }
    ]
  }'
```

### Check for incomplete multipart uploads
```bash
aws s3api list-multipart-uploads --bucket <harbor-bucket-name>
```

### Monitor S3 storage reduction over time
```bash
# Quick size check script — run before and after GC
echo "=== $(date) ===" >> /tmp/harbor_s3_sizes.log
aws s3 ls s3://<harbor-bucket-name> --recursive --summarize \
  | tail -3 >> /tmp/harbor_s3_sizes.log
```

